WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.540
DAN:
-Hey, kids! It's everybody's favorite

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:03.140
whipping boy of the critical term!

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:05.240
It's ludonarrative dissonance.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.000
Off the top here, this is ultimately going to be

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.300
a defense of ludonarrative dissonance

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:17.760
as a critical concept,

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.960
as a tool for descriptive criticism.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:22.620
But first, a bit of backstory.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.640
"Ludonarrative dissonance" as a term

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.020
was coined by Clint Hocking

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.140
in a 2007 blogpost titled

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.360
"Ludonarrative Dissonance in Bioshock:

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.580
The problem of what the game is about."

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.300
In the post, Clint argues that there is a disconnect

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.500
between the story told by

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.140
the game's mechanical interactions

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.100
and the story told by the game's narrative interaction,

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.820
that while the game appears to thematically criticize

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.880
Randian Objectivism as a foundational philosophy

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.800
and works this criticism into the game's interactions,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.780
the same doesn't hold true for the game's fiction.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:56.000
Quote, "Yet in the game's fiction on the other hand,

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.320
I do not have that freedom to choose

00:00:58.320 --> 00:00:59.960
between helping Atlas or not.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.960
Under the ludic contract,

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.860
if I accept to adopt an Objectivist approach,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.560
I can harvest Little Sisters.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.920
If I reject that approach, I can rescue them.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.240
Under the story, if I reject an Objectivist approach,

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.480
I can help Atlas and oppose Ryan,

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:18.640
and if I choose to adopt an Objectivist approach –

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.640
well too bad… I can stop playing the game,

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.320
but that's about it."

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.680
This idea of pointing out when a game's mechanics

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.980
are being dissonant with the game's fiction

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:32.440
enjoyed a brief period where it was all the rage,

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:35.200
the hot, new thing to point to in games,

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.560
and subsequent to that, it also saw a pushback

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.320
and reevaluation of its value as a concept.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:42.900
On his series "Errant Signal,"

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.800
Chris Franklin, AKA Campster,

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.960
described it this way in his 2015 video,

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:49.880
"The Debate That Never Took Place."

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.060
CHRIS:
-Like, story and play both exist

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:53.420
in service to the overall work,

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.960
not as two forces in conflict with one another.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.240
So... why do we frame them that way?

00:01:58.240 --> 00:01:59.780
It'd be like going to the movies,

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:01.520
and then, afterwards, talking with friends

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.220
about how the film worked as a story,

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:04.720
and then talking about how the film worked

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.520
as an example of cinematography,

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.600
but never at the same time.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.120
You'd never do that,

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:12.260
and definitely not in a way that would posit a film

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.140
as "cinemanarratively dissonant."

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.660
DAN:
-Now, I love that video,

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.100
and I love that clip in particular

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.360
for a couple reasons.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.480
First, that video as a whole makes a great point

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.380
about the need for games criticism

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:27.100
to accept games as a whole text,

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:28.960
that we should evaluate games

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.580
not as a collection of systems on one side

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.580
and a collection of fiction on the other,

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.240
but as a coherent whole

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.760
that either works or doesn't.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.620
In particular, Chris is concerned

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:43.100
with this approach to games criticism,

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.580
especially the forms of games criticism

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.180
that exist in reviews,

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:50.200
that very much compartmentalize the two faces

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:52.760
of the game in a way that will lead to discussing games

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.380
as "Great gameplay, but the story sucks!"

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.540
or "Great story! Shame about the gameplay!"

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:01.060
And on this front, I should say yes!

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:03.700
I agree. We should, as a baseline,

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:05.480
treat games as a whole text.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.780
If the story is bad and doesn't set up coherent themes

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.300
or if it is ugly, dehumanizing, and insulting,

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.460
then that should count as a mark against the game,

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:19.020
no matter how fun it is to bend bullets.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.860
However, in calling for that sort of holistic approach,

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:24.580
critics have inadvertently tossed

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:26.120
the baby out with the bathwater,

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.760
which brings me to the second thing

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.960
that I loved about that previous clip.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.580
Chris has, in setting up a counterexample,

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:36.540
accidentally created a great mechanism for criticism,

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.340
because there absolutely are films that

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.460
I would describe as "cinemanarratively dissonant."

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:44.380
"Transformers," for one.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:45.880
By the text of the script

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:47.600
for the two films that she is in,

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.200
the character of Mikaela Banes is smart, funny,

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:54.400
talented, driven, hard-working, quick-thinking,

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.040
courageous, and mature.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:57.860
She is an active agent in the plot,

00:03:57.860 --> 00:03:59.160
she comes up with plans,

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.940
and her personal arc meshes with the rest of the film.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:04.320
The simple fact that she is a mechanic

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.340
gives her more thematic connection

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:09.660
to the Transformers than anything going on with Sam.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:13.820
However, the camera treats Mikaela as a piece of meat.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:17.180
People perceive her as being superfluous eye candy,

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:19.560
as nagging, as irrational,

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.860
because that is how she is consistently framed.

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:26.620
The cinematography is not, strictly speaking, bad.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:30.140
It is, in its own sphere, competent and well-executed.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:34.060
Mikaela's writing simultaneously is not bad,

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:37.220
but they are not in agreement with each other.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:39.800
There is cinemanarrative dissonance there.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:43.720
The story told by the camera does not agree

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.080
with the one being told by the script.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:47.880
I actually think that right there

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.840
is a great conceptual tool for critics to use

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.720
to describe why something that is

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:55.900
technically proficient on the whole isn't working,

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:57.520
and within games criticism,

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:00.380
I think the idea of ludonarrative dissonance

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:02.820
is a valuable tool that complements

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:04.880
a holistic approach to games criticism

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:06.800
rather than detracts from it

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:08.900
because it makes it easier to describe

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:11.140
when the story is telling one message

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.060
and the mechanics are telling another.

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:14.260
Now, taken as a whole,

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:17.740
this kind of dissonance is less common in film.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:19.420
Film as a medium is more mature,

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:21.300
the language is more established,

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.940
and the production methods have generally worked out

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.760
most of the kinks in terms of getting everyone involved

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.680
on the same page in terms of theme and tone.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.620
That's not so much the case in games.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:36.260
Even asking about the themes of gameplay,

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:39.460
the story told by mechanical interactions,

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:41.860
is still a really new idea.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:43.980
Most filmmakers today have learned

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.240
the grammar of film by virtue of growing up

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.280
watching films made by filmmakers

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.120
who grew up watching films made by filmmakers,

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:53.840
who's tinkered around asking themselves questions

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:56.180
about theme and story and structure and tone.

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.420
With video games, we are right now still

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:02.400
in that first wave of creators and critics and readers

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.300
who are asking questions like

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:06.480
"What story is the gameplay telling?,"

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:08.680
and because games are often made

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:10.520
in compartmentalized ways

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:13.900
with the writers over here and the designers over there,

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.220
it is simply more common for

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:19.320
the two disciplines to tell different stories.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:21.380
Clint's example still stands.

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:23.800
It's not that the fiction of Bioshock is bad

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:25.740
or that the gameplay is awful,

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:28.100
but simply that the two aren't

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:29.900
in agreement with each other.

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:32.500
To me, the idea of ludonarrative dissonance

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:35.040
gives us a way to acknowledge when both teams

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.640
have done their jobs well in isolation,

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:40.740
but have, perhaps, failed to support each other,

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:44.620
or whoever has led the project has failed to keep

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:48.640
everyone pointed towards the same thematic/tonal goal

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:49.620
So what do you think?

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:50.640
Let me know in the comments

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:52.480
if you think the idea of dissonance is

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.300
a valuable critical tool for descriptive criticism,

00:06:55.300 --> 00:07:00.100
or are they just, like, games, man?

